= Mental simulation =

== Overview ==

{{:Mental simulation/Simple}}

'''Mental simulation''' refers to cognitive processes used to construct representations of events and actions beyond current perception. This umbrella includes prospective imagination, counterfactual reconstruction and rehearsal, each with distinct experimental literatures and explanatory constraints.

== Core research distinctions ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Research sense !! Typical question !! Evidentiary limitation
|-
| Episodic future simulation || Can someone imagine a novel, personally situated future episode? || Subjective vividness does not establish predictive accuracy.
|-
| Counterfactual simulation || How might a past outcome differ under an altered antecedent? || Imagined alternatives are not observed interventions.
|-
| Simulation heuristic || Does ease of mental construction influence judgments? || Accessibility and real-world probability can diverge.
|-
| Motor or action rehearsal || Does mental practice improve performance under defined conditions? || Effects vary across tasks and training protocols.
|}

Kahneman and Tversky's simulation heuristic concerned imagined alternatives and their influence on judgment. Schacter, Addis and Buckner analyzed episodic memory and future simulation in cognitive neuroscience. These are related programs of research rather than identical measurements of one universal faculty.<ref name="heuristic_core">Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky, [https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/judgment-under-uncertainty/simulation-heuristic/9C6ED91A6429A8443641641DC41A52FE “The simulation heuristic”] (1982).</ref><ref name="brain_core">Daniel L. Schacter, Donna Rose Addis and Randy L. Buckner, [https://doi.org/10.1038/nrn2213 “Remembering the past to imagine the future: the prospective brain”], ''Nature Reviews Neuroscience'' 8, 657–661 (2007).</ref>

== Scientific interpretation ==

A valid experiment specifies whether it measures generated scenarios, subjective ease, behavioral predictions, memory details, decision outcomes or neural signals. These observables are not interchangeable. A participant's ability to describe a vivid scenario cannot establish its objective likelihood or prove a corresponding event is causally possible.

Mental simulation relates to [[Related to::Cognition|cognition]], [[Related to::Reasoning|reasoning]], [[Related to::Decision theory|decision making]] and [[Related to::Metacognition|metacognitive judgment]]. The subject also intersects with [[Related to::Spotlight effect|self-referential social estimates]], but a spotlight-effect experiment is not itself a general theory of event simulation.

== Research use and limitations ==

Studies should report stimulus selection, baseline comparison, individual variability, scenario coding, blinding, demand characteristics, behavioral outcomes and uncertainty. The failure of an imagined scenario to occur does not prove the cognitive process was useless; an accurate probability forecast is different from predicting one realized outcome.

AI planning systems can generate candidate trajectories in explicit computational models. That analogy alone does not identify human neural imagery or prove consciousness; agent-specific measurements and mechanisms remain necessary.

